236
00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:47.160
why their offending has happened.

237
00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:49.720
So the really common ones are that they're young,

238
00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:53.000
so a youth discount usually can be applied

239
00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:57.000
to someone who's under the age of 25 roughly,

240
00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:59.240
and that's based on sort of what we understand

241
00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:01.840
about neuroscience, that the brain is still developing,

242
00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:05.520
that there's a higher likelihood of impulsivity,

243
00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:10.480
lack of consequential thinking and all of those things that can show up when someone is

244
00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:15.200
offending when they're younger. It also recognises that younger people are more likely to be able

245
00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:21.040
to be rehabilitated. The other ones are you know they've never been in trouble before,

246
00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:26.160
they have no previous convictions, they come from a really rough background and again this

247
00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:32.000
recognises what we know about neurodevelopment that you know your pathways are being formed

248
00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:37.280
right from the time you're born and if you've been exposed to violence, exposed to drugs,

249
00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:41.680
you've come from a background where there's been neglect, you've come from a background where

250
00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:47.600
offending has been normalised. You've been sent on a path that's not your choosing and so the courts

251
00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:54.560
recognise that they probably do need to be at least an allowance made for the fact that this person

252
00:16:54.560 --> 00:17:00.240
has ended up where they are versus someone who's come from a perfectly normal childhood,

253
00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:06.680
has had every opportunity, perhaps has, you know, a really secure financial

254
00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:12.760
position and still chooses to go and behave in an anti-social way. It can be

255
00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:19.720
very difficult, I'm sure, for victims of these crimes to see people getting discounts, right?

256
00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:23.640
So do they get a say in all of this? They do. And so one of the things that

257
00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:27.000
they have to balance that against is the impact on the victim and that is also

258
00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:33.040
in there in the sentencing act. The way that the court hears about that is often

259
00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:37.320
through what's called a victim impact statement and this is where victims are

260
00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:43.280
really given a say in the actual court process so they provide a statement to

261
00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:48.880
the court ahead of sentencing. The judge reads it, the person can choose to read

262
00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:55.440
it out to themselves or have it read by a prosecutor or the police officer in

263
00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:03.740
charge of the case and that tells the judge what the impact has been on them. So it might

264
00:18:03.740 --> 00:18:09.780
be things like the counselling that's still being needed, ongoing physical rehabilitation

265
00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:17.660
if it's been a violent act, the mental health impacts, the impacts on other relationships,

266
00:18:17.660 --> 00:18:24.540
the loss of trust and other people, and an ability to work the stress of the actual court

267
00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:29.500
process and that brings us back to another discount that I think people must understand

268
00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:34.380
and that's the guilty plea discount. So the earlier that you plead guilty, the bigger the

269
00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:39.980
discount, so it starts at 25% if you plead guilty basically at the earliest opportunity

270
00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:45.420
and then it drops away as you get further through the court process. That recognises

271
00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:52.940
A, the cost of taking things to trial, it recognises the impact on the victim,

272
00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:58.940
the amount of court time that's sucked up, getting prepared for a trial and all of those things.

273
00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:04.940
And it provides an incentive for people to put up their hand and not just go,

274
00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:07.940
oh, well, we'll just take it to trial and see what happens.

275
00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:08.940
I've got nothing to lose.

276
00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:09.940
Yeah, exactly right.

277
00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:12.940
Ultimately, what is sentencing trying to achieve?

278
00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:14.940
And I think this is the key question.

279
00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:18.940
And I know it's a tricky one to answer because it's a balancing act.

280
00:19:18.940 --> 00:19:25.940
But to me, it's the key question because when we are raising concerns about giving people

281
00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:32.940
discounts for things, oftentimes we're thinking purely punitively.

282
00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:37.940
But when you stop and think, okay, what is the purpose of sentencing?

283
00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:40.940
What are we trying to achieve with our justice system?

284
00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:43.940
There are other things that play. Can you talk a little to that?

285
00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:49.220
you know, you have the denunciation, which is basically us saying this isn't behaviour

286
00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:51.420
that we as a community tolerate.

287
00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:57.060
The deterrence is, you know, you sort of, you're hoping that all other people will see this and

288
00:19:57.060 --> 00:20:00.820
go, oh no, I don't want to, I don't want to be set to prison for that amount of time,

289
00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:03.460
so I won't, I won't behave in that way.

290
00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:10.220
But then we do have, as part of our sentence, saying this idea that it should assist in

291
00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:16.540
a defendant's rehabilitation, because ultimately we aren't keeping people in prison forever

292
00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:22.860
and then just locking them in and throwing away the key. They do have to be released. And so

293
00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:30.060
finding a way to balance the sentence that is going to somehow assist in that rehabilitation

294
00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:37.020
not end up with people who are just institutionalised. One of the concerned people judges have about

295
00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:41.340
sending particularly young people to prison is that you're sending a young person into an

296
00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:46.380
environment where they're going to be mixing with older people who are more set in their criminal

297
00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:52.060
ways and is that really what we want our young people doing is is there another way and so

298
00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:58.620
all of that has to be taken into account and that's where with young people in particular

299
00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:05.580
judges are more inclined to impose non-custodial sentences. So it means not necessarily prison.

300
00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:11.340
not necessarily present. So we do have some other sentences that are available to judges

301
00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:17.100
if a term in imprisonment is of two years or less. So the one people probably most familiar with

302
00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:26.940
is home detention. That's where you basically just got to stay at home 24-7 and it's to a specific

303
00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:33.020
address. Then we have community detention that's also an electronically monitored sentence like

304
00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:38.060
home detention where someone's wearing a bracelet at judges less keen on that one because sometimes

305
00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:43.900
referred to as the punishment to sleep in your own bed at night as a normally an overnight curfew

306
00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:50.620
and so you can go out during the day but then you're at home overnight. Then there's things

307
00:21:50.620 --> 00:21:55.420
like community work with you know going out putting out rubbish that kind of thing that

308
00:21:55.420 --> 00:22:01.340
I'm sure there's a lot more options or community work sentences but that's the general idea

309
00:22:01.340 --> 00:22:06.700
is, you know, giving back to the community in some way, supervision, and then there are

310
00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:07.700
fines.

311
00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:08.700
Yeah, right.

312
00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:12.500
So there's a real mixture of the area of options, and also I guess you sometimes hear

313
00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:17.780
people comparing sentences of what can look like the same criminal activity.

314
00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:23.300
One person can rob a bank and end up with this sentence, and someone else can do a very

315
00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:29.460
similar crime and end up with a much harsher sentence, and all the things that we've discussed

316
00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:33.900
today are the reasons why these sentences can differ.

317
00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:37.540
Yes, and there's huge variables in terms of the actual offending,

318
00:22:37.540 --> 00:22:40.740
what happened, but then as we've talked about massive differences

319
00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:46.220
and who these people are and what circumstances are particular

320
00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:49.140
to them, their age, their background, all the rest of it,

321
00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:52.340
and sort of the circumstances around their offending.

322
00:22:52.340 --> 00:22:56.380
And judges are given a whole lot of information about that.

323
00:22:56.380 --> 00:22:59.500
Most of it, we don't see, you might not know,

324
00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:02.580
there's psychological reports, there's background reports,

325
00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:04.500
there's support leaders from the community,

326
00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:05.900
there's leaders of remorse,

327
00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:09.420
there's all these things that go into it.

328
00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:13.860
And again, so much information about what actually happened

329
00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:16.220
and the events and the circumstances of it.

330
00:23:16.220 --> 00:23:18.940
And that part of the process is usually,

331
00:23:18.940 --> 00:23:20.580
there's a bit more transparency,

332
00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:21.980
but all that other stuff,

333
00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:23.820
there's a lot of private information in there

334
00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:28.380
that, you know, members of the public aren't necessarily entitled to, but the judges taking

335
00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:29.380
into account.

336
00:23:29.380 --> 00:23:32.540
So we just have to trust that they know what they're doing and that they're doing the best

337
00:23:32.540 --> 00:23:33.540
thing for all of us.

338
00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:34.540
Sure.

339
00:23:34.540 --> 00:23:40.100
And the judges balancing that thing of keeping the community safe and holding this person

340
00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:45.860
to account and hopefully imposing a sentence that somehow meets both of those things and

341
00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:51.660
helps this person start to learn from their mistakes to become rehabilitated and move

342
00:23:51.660 --> 00:23:52.820
on in a positive way.

343
00:23:52.820 --> 00:23:55.660
Yeah, so future-proofing the community safety as well,

344
00:23:55.660 --> 00:23:57.860
not just keeping them safe while someone's incarcerated,

345
00:23:57.860 --> 00:23:59.780
but thinking about when their person comes out,

346
00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:01.300
what their prospects are going to be like.

347
00:24:01.300 --> 00:24:03.420
Yes. Hey, thank you so very much, Hannah.

348
00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:04.900
It was lovely to chat with you again.

349
00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:06.580
Really appreciate your time.

350
00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:08.220
That's being case by case.

351
00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:11.100
An Open Justice podcast brought to you by the New Zealand Herald

352
00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:12.900
with thanks to New Zealand on air.
